Q: Parse the free format address to save into the database A: <p>For US addresses, this is a solved problem in 2026. Use an address parsing API that handles the ambiguity for you.</p>
<p>For example, with <a href="https://sthan.io" rel="nofollow noreferrer">sthan.io</a></p>
<pre class="lang-none prettyprint-override"><code>GET https://api.sthan.io/AddressParser/USA/Single/1600%20Pennsylvania%20Ave%20NW%20Washington%20DC%2020500
Authorization: Bearer sthan_test_your_api_key
</code></pre>
<p>Response:</p>
<pre class="lang-json prettyprint-override"><code>{
  &quot;result&quot;: {
    &quot;inputAddress&quot;: &quot;1600 Pennsylvania Ave NW Washington DC 20500&quot;,
    &quot;fullAddress&quot;: &quot;1600 Pennsylvania Ave NW, Washington, DC 20500&quot;,
    &quot;addressLine1&quot;: &quot;1600 Pennsylvania Ave NW&quot;,
    &quot;addressLine2&quot;: &quot;&quot;,
    &quot;addressNumber&quot;: &quot;1600&quot;,
    &quot;streetName&quot;: &quot;Pennsylvania&quot;,
    &quot;streetPreDir&quot;: &quot;&quot;,
    &quot;streetPostType&quot;: &quot;Ave&quot;,
    &quot;streetPostDir&quot;: &quot;NW&quot;,
    &quot;unitType&quot;: &quot;&quot;,
    &quot;unitNumber&quot;: &quot;&quot;,
    &quot;city&quot;: &quot;Washington&quot;,
    &quot;stateCode&quot;: &quot;DC&quot;,
    &quot;state&quot;: &quot;District of Columbia&quot;,
    &quot;zipCode&quot;: &quot;20500&quot;,
    &quot;zip4&quot;: &quot;0005&quot;,
    &quot;county&quot;: &quot;District of Columbia&quot;,
    &quot;isPostalVerified&quot;: true,
    &quot;confidence&quot;: 0.97,
    &quot;algorithmUsed&quot;: &quot;100&quot;
  }
}
</code></pre>
<p>In C#:</p>
<pre class="lang-cs prettyprint-override"><code>using var client = new HttpClient();
client.DefaultRequestHeaders.Authorization =
    new AuthenticationHeaderValue(&quot;Bearer&quot;, &quot;sthan_test_your_api_key&quot;);

var address = Uri.EscapeDataString(textArea.Text);
var response = await client.GetFromJsonAsync&lt;ApiResponse&lt;ParsedAddress&gt;&gt;(
    $&quot;https://api.sthan.io/AddressParser/USA/Single/{address}&quot;);

var parsed = response.Result;

// Save to database
record.AddressLine1 = parsed.AddressLine1;   // &quot;1600 Pennsylvania Ave NW&quot;
record.AddressLine2 = parsed.AddressLine2;   // &quot;&quot; (or unit/apt info)
record.City          = parsed.City;           // &quot;Washington&quot;
record.State         = parsed.StateCode;      // &quot;DC&quot;
record.ZipCode       = parsed.ZipCode;        // &quot;20500&quot;
record.Zip4          = parsed.Zip4;           // &quot;0005&quot;
record.County        = parsed.County;         // &quot;District of Columbia&quot;
</code></pre>
<p>The API handles all the messy edge cases: missing ZIP codes, misspelled cities, abbreviated street types (<code>St</code> vs <code>Street</code>), directionals (<code>NW</code>, <code>S</code>), unit/apartment parsing, etc. It returns a <code>confidence</code> score (0.0-1.0) so you can flag low-confidence parses for human review.</p>
<p>The other answers are right that <strong>rolling your own parser is painful</strong> but you don't have to. If you need the addresses verified against postal records (not just parsed), the same service has a verification endpoint at <code>/AddressVerification/Usa/Single/{address}</code> that adds DPV confirmation and ZIP+4.</p>
